+    section_hash: 525d528d50e1402359267db76ad15ae3d3eee1e303c1acb29b520d54d902a68a
+    summary: Array Shapes
+    text: "### Array Shapes
+
+
+      AudioSig accepts arrays in these shapes:
+
+
+      - **1D**: `(samples,)` - Single channel audio
+
+      - **2D**: `(channels, samples)` - Multi-channel audio
+
+      - **nD**: `(..., samples)` - Batch dimensions followed by sample axis
+
+
+      The sample axis defaults to the last dimension (`axis=-1`)."
+    links: []
+  - section_id: md:section:docs/getting-started.md::data-types
+    doc_path: docs/getting-started.md
+    heading_path:
+      - Getting Started with AudioSig
+      - Understanding the API
+      - Data types
+    heading_slug: data-types
+    line_span:
+      - 154
+      - 163
+    section_hash: 9eee05638c32148d6d509abbe761ca65218f068269d5d325280e344c9850e0f1
+    summary: Data types
+    text:
+      "### Data types\n\n- Audio inputs must be `float32` or `float64` NumPy arrays.\n\
+      - Audio transforms such as gain, resampling, time stretching, pitch shifting,\n\
+      \  and trimming preserve the audio dtype.\n- Feature and decibel functions may\
+      \ return `float32` or `float64` as documented.\n- VAD functions return boolean\
+      \ masks.\n- Interval and frame-index conversion functions return integer values.\n\
+      - Public operations do not mutate the input audio."
+    links: []
+  - section_id: md:section:docs/getting-started.md::time-stretching
+    doc_path: docs/getting-started.md
+    heading_path:
+      - Getting Started with AudioSig
+      - Understanding the API
+      - Time Stretching
+    heading_slug: time-stretching
+    line_span:
+      - 164
+      - 181
+    section_hash: e9ea3d312f7d09da814104d58db10a85fc6185d65186bc5df8ca31d06e40b313
+    summary: Time Stretching
+    text: "### Time Stretching
+
+
+      `time_stretch(audio, rate)` changes duration while preserving pitch:
+
+
+      - `rate > 1.0`: Faster and shorter
+
+      - `rate < 1.0`: Slower and longer
+
+      - `rate = 1.0`: No change (returns copy)
+
+
+      ```python
+
+      from audiosig import time_stretch
+
+
+      # Make audio 50% faster
+
+      faster = time_stretch(audio, rate=1.5)
+
+
+      # Make audio 30% slower
+
+      slower = time_stretch(audio, rate=0.7)
+
+      ```"
+    links: []
+  - section_id: md:section:docs/getting-started.md::pitch-shifting
+    doc_path: docs/getting-started.md
+    heading_path:
+      - Getting Started with AudioSig
+      - Understanding the API
+      - Pitch Shifting
+    heading_slug: pitch-shifting
+    line_span:
+      - 182
+      - 220
+    section_hash: 32cde56eb2b4e274c7bf8b811f0b472782d64e786a95bbd4823a9f4ca5693dcd
+    summary: Pitch Shifting
+    text:
+      "### Pitch Shifting\n\n`pitch_shift(audio, sample_rate, semitones)` changes\
+      \ pitch while preserving duration:\n\n- `semitones > 0`: Higher pitch\n- `semitones\
+      \ < 0`: Lower pitch\n- `semitones = 0`: No change (returns copy)\n\n```python\n\
+      from audiosig import pitch_shift\n\n# Raise pitch by 3 semitones\nhigher = pitch_shift(audio,\
+      \ sample_rate=24000, semitones=3.0)\n\n# Lower pitch by 5 semitones\nlower = pitch_shift(audio,\
+      \ sample_rate=24000, semitones=-5.0)\n```\n\nFor experimental speech-only direct\
+      \ pitch/prosody processing, opt in with\n`method=\"td_psola\"`. Moderate changes\
+      \ only are supported; unvoiced regions are\nduration-scaled without pitch synthesis\
+      \ and formant preservation is not\nguaranteed:\n\n```python\nfrom audiosig import\
+      \ apply_speech_effects, pitch_shift\n\nvoice_up = pitch_shift(audio, sample_rate=24_000,\
+      \ semitones=4.0, method=\"td_psola\")\nslower_voice = apply_speech_effects(\n\
+      \    audio,\n    sample_rate=24_000,\n    rate=0.85,\n    semitones=-3.0,\n  \
+      \  method=\"td_psola\",\n)\n```\n\nTD-PSOLA is experimental and intended for clean\
+      \ or mildly noisy speech, not\nmusic, polyphony, strong noise, reverb, creaky\
+      \ voice, or extreme shifts."
+    links: []
